## Description

The `webhook-lifecycle-complete` rule accepts inline functions. It rejects lifecycle methods that reference a function by identifier.

The n8n type allows a function reference. The runtime also supports it. It reads the function from `webhookMethods` and calls it with the hook context.

## Reproduction

```typescript
async function checkExists(this: IHookFunctions): Promise<boolean> {
	return true;
}

async function create(this: IHookFunctions): Promise<boolean> {
	return true;
}

async function deleteWebhook(this: IHookFunctions): Promise<boolean> {
	return true;
}

webhookMethods = {
	default: {
		checkExists,
		create,
		delete: deleteWebhook,
	},
};
```

The rule reports all three methods as missing. The runtime can call all three methods.

## Expected behavior

The rule must accept an identifier value as an implemented lifecycle method.

This issue is separate from the static property-key fix in #34972.

## Bug Description

When a node configured with `onError: continueErrorOutput` fails, `WorkflowExecute.handleNodeErrorOutput()` builds the error item by calling `dataProxy.$getPairedItem(...)`. That call can **throw** (`createMissingPairedItemError`), but the surrounding code only handles the `null` return. The throw therefore escapes `handleNodeErrorOutput` entirely, and three things go wrong at once:

1. **The error output never fires.** The node's error branch does not execute — in my case a backup-AI agent wired to output 1 never ran.
2. **The node's *input* item is emitted on main output 0**, as though it were the node's result. Downstream nodes treat it as a normal success item.
3. The node's `runData` records `executionStatus: "error"`, while the execution as a whole finishes `success`, so nothing surfaces the failure.

In my case the leaked input item happened to carry a field with the same name as the agent's reply field (`output`), so a downstream IF node's "not empty" check passed and an internal routing label was sent verbatim to an end user.

This is distinct from #30050. That issue is about *user expressions* (`$('Node').item`) failing downstream when an error output is used. Here it is n8n's own error-routing code that throws, so the error output is never populated at all.

### The code

`packages/core/src/execution-engine/workflow-execute.ts` — `handleNodeErrorOutput`, unchanged as of `n8n@2.36.9`:

```ts
if (executionData.source === null || pairedItemData === undefined) {
  // Source data is missing for some reason so we can not figure out the item
  errorItems.push(item);                       // <-- safe fallback already exists
} else {
  const pairedItemInputIndex = pairedItemData.input || 0;
  const sourceData = executionData.source[NodeConnectionTypes.Main][pairedItemInputIndex];

  const constPairedItem = dataProxy.$getPairedItem(   // <-- can THROW
    sourceData!.previousNode,
    sourceData,
    pairedItemData,
  );

  if (constPairedItem === null) {               // <-- only the null return is handled
    errorItems.push(item);
  } else {
    errorItems.push({ ...item, json: { ...constPairedItem.json, ...item.json } });
  }
}
```

`packages/workflow/src/workflow-data-proxy.ts` — `getPairedItem` throws rather than returning `null` (two sites, ~L1054 and ~L1064):

```ts
throw createMissingPairedItemError(sourceData.previousNode, usedMethodName);
```

### Observed stack trace

```
ExpressionError: Paired item data for $getPairedItem from node 'Switch' is unavailable.
Ensure 'Switch' is providing the required output.
    at createMissingPairedItemError (n8n-workflow/src/workflow-data-proxy.ts:869:11)
    at Proxy.getPairedItem          (n8n-workflow/src/workflow-data-proxy.ts:1015:12)
    at WorkflowExecute.handleNodeErrorOutput (n8n-core/src/execution-engine/workflow-execute.ts:2752:41)
```

The third frame is the important one: this is not a user expression being evaluated, it is the error-output router.

### Why the lookup failed

The failing node is fed from a Switch node. The item carried `pairedItem: { item: 0 }` with **no `input` key**, so `pairedItemInputIndex` fell back to `0` and `executionData.source.main[0]` was used. The recorded source entry was:

```json
{ "previousNode": "<Switch>", "previousNodeOutput": 0, "previousNodeRun": 0 }
```

while the data had actually been emitted on that Switch's output index **2**. Output 0 was empty, so the traceback found no item and threw. Whether the mis-recorded `previousNodeOutput` is itself a second bug I can't say, but `handleNodeErrorOutput` should survive it either way.

## To Reproduce

I want to be upfront: **I could not build a minimal reproduction.** On the affected workflow it fires roughly 1 run in 8 with identical input. I built seven throwaway workflows trying to isolate it and every one routed to the error output correctly:

1. Switch with two outputs wired to the same failing Code node
2. Switch with the fallback folded into a rule output (single connection)
3. The exact edge shape of the affected workflow, using Code nodes
4. Switch outputs only, no upstream error edge
5. Upstream error edge only, fallback folded
6. A real `@n8n/n8n-nodes-langchain.agent` forced to fail via a nonexistent model
7. The same agent with a Postgres chat-memory sub-node attached

In all seven, `source.previousNodeOutput` was recorded correctly and the error output fired. The trigger appears to require `source.previousNodeOutput` to disagree with the branch the data was actually emitted on, which I have not found a way to force.

The affected workflow is a LangChain agent (`typeVersion 3.1`, `returnIntermediateSteps: true`, Postgres chat memory, four `toolWorkflow` tools) fed from a Switch node that has four outputs, two of which are wired to that agent, plus a third incoming edge from the error output of an upstream agent.

## Expected behavior

`handleNodeErrorOutput` should be defensive. Failing to *decorate* an error item with its paired input JSON is cosmetic; it must never prevent the error output from firing, and it must never cause the input item to continue down the success output.

The safe fallback already exists a few lines above — `errorItems.push(item)`. A `try`/`catch` around the lookup would be enough:

```ts
let constPairedItem: INodeExecutionData | null = null;
try {
  constPairedItem = dataProxy.$getPairedItem(sourceData!.previousNode, sourceData, pairedItemData);
} catch {
  constPairedItem = null;   // cannot resolve the input item; still route to the error output
}

if (constPairedItem === null) {
  errorItems.push(item);
} else {
  errorItems.push({ ...item, json: { ...constPairedItem.json, ...item.json } });
}
```

Silently emitting the *input* item on the success output is the dangerous part — it looks like a valid result to every downstream node.

## Debug Info

n8n 2.27.4 (verified the same code is present at tag `n8n@2.36.9`).

- **Operating System:** Ubuntu 24.04 LTS (host); n8n container on Alpine 3.22
- **n8n Version:** 2.27.4
- **Node.js Version:** 24.16.0
- **Database:** SQLite
- **Execution mode:** main (regular)
- **Hosting:** Self-hosted (Docker)
